README
Written by: Jerry Qiao
Gerp project
Date started: Jul 26 2026

Purpose: 
    Gerp allows the user to find words in a directory. If a word is 
    found in a directory, Gerp will write out the location of that
    word in an file that the user provides. Both case sensitive
    and insensitve word query options are availble in Gerp.

Acknowledgement:
    Thanks to Mavis and Max for providing insight and suggestions.

How to compile and run:
    Compile command:  make
    Run command:      ./gerp inputDirectory outputFile

List of files:
    main.cpp: 
        Driver program for gerp. Checks to make sure the user has
        enter the right run command before starting gerp.
    
    Gerp.h: 
        Gerp class is the main functionality of gerp. Gerp will index 
        the content of the user-provided directory and will prepare to 
        handle queries about the location of words in that directory.
        Gerp class also allows user to change the file where gerp produces
        its output.
    
    Gerp.cpp:
        Implementation file for Gerp class.
    
    Table.h:
        Table is a class designed to mimic the functions of cpp 
        <unorderd_map> that I wrote for gerp. Table does not support
        deleting elements. This file also contains the implementation
        function for Table class.
    
    processing.h:
        the directory traversing functions in gerp is a modified version
        of the traverseDirectory() function in this file. Gerp.cpp also 
        uses the stripNonAlphaNum() functions in its implementation.

    processing.cpp: 
        implementation file for processing.h

    unit_tests.h:
        Used to unit test overall functionality and edge cases of 
        processing.h functions, Table class, and gerp.
    
    try.cpp:
        design for more in-depth and customizable testing of certain
        unit_test functions.

    Makefile:
        streamlines the compilation of code

    README: 
        This file.


Architectural Overview:
    main.cpp:
        driver program for gerp. After verifying run command, calls Gerp.
    
    Gerp:
        main functionality of gerp. Indexes and traverses through the 
        directory using FSTree and DirNode classes. The data from
        the directory is then stored using Table. Also calls processing.h
        functions to clean up strings.
    
    FSTree:
        FSTree, when given a directory name, will represent the directory
        as a tree of DirNodes

    DirNode:
        A node in FSTree that represents a single directory. Gerp uses
        DirNode to traverse through the files and subdirectories in a
        DirNode

    Table:
        A data structure designed to mimic cpp's <unordered_map>. When
        used by Gerp, Table takes a word as its key and returns the 
        mapped value, which contains information on where this word
        can be found. Table maintains a no duplicate invariant for
        all mapped values of a certain key. This is done through
        cpp <set>, though I would really have liked to use
        cpp <unordered_set> instead for the faster access and insertion

    processing.h:
        This file contains a helpful function: stripNonAlphaNum(). Gerp
        use this function to clean up the queried word and the words in
        the user-provided directory.
    
Data Structure used:
    I used list to represent all the files and the lines inside that file.
    My reasons are the following: random access is not important; I would
    only insert through pushback; there is no need for deletion; list uses
    less space than vector. Due to all of these reasons, I think list is
    the simplest and most space efficient data structure to contain 
    information about the files and the lines.

    I used two massive Tables to store all the words encountered in a
    directory. A hash-table like data structure makes the most sense 
    for a project like gerp, where you query a word (the key) and
    the code is supposed to return the location where that word is 
    found (the map). Since the unordered series of cpp data structure is
    banned, I built my own. Table is designed to mimic an unordered map.
    Table does not support deletion since Gerp doesn't need it. It uses
    linear probing because I had some trouble with quadratic probing not
    going through all the data slots before it timed out. 

    The actual data of Table is stored in a vector for its flexible
    resizing and random access properties.

    Table maintains a no duplicate invariant for
    all mapped values of a certain key. This is done through
    cpp <set>, though I would really have liked to use
    cpp <unordered_set> instead for the faster access and insertion.
    I considered building an unordered set of my own, but I felt like
    it was too much of a hassle. Plus, my program already passed the
    baseline requirements, although it is a bit slow for my liking.

Algorithm:
    The overall flow of the gerp is that main() runs, main() calls
    gerp, and gerp handles the rest.

    In gerp, the alorithm can be split into 2 major parts: indexing
    the data and handing query.

    To index the data, gerp first runs through all the files to record
    the file names. Then, it runs through all the files again to record
    the lines. Uh, now that I think about it, these two functions can
    probably be done in 1 pass through. I made this design choice originally
    because I was worried about vectors deleting pointers when it resized.
    But since i've moved to a list structure, this can probably be simplified.
    Anyways, afterwards, gerp runs through all the recorded lines and stores 
    the words in two big Tables. One table stores all the case sensitive words
    and their location. The other does the same for the case insensitive ones.

    For query handling, most of them are simple except for search().
    Search is complicated because I had to built a whole data container
    for this. Search calls the find() function within the tables
    using a user provided string as the key, it then sends the results
    off to writeFile() to be written to the user-provided outfile.

    I think I will also talk a bit about Table's algorithm, since that
    is also a big part of the project.

    Table works as a hash table with its key and map stored in the array. 
    Its main functionalities are insert() and find(). 

    With insert, it first check if the load factor is appropriate.
    If not, it makes reserves more size in the data vector and re-copies
    everything in. Afterwards, insert hashes the key, makes sure there
    is no collision, and puts the key and its map in that slot in the
    data vector.

    For find(), it hashes the key and see if it can find the key stored
    in data vector. It will go through every element in data before it
    gives up. After finding the right map, it returns a set of 
    whatever is stored there. If it can't find it, it returns an empty set.
    I am using a set to ensure there is no duplicates in the mapped value.

Testing:
    Learning from my horrific testing in typewriter, I tried my best
    to adhere to the "write a little, test a little". After writing each
    class or a major function, I test it. All the testing can be found
    in unit_tests.h.

    In chronological order:

    processing.h:
        the functions were relatively simple. I wrote and tested one
        function first before doing the same for the second function.

    Table.h:
        Wrote and test in this order: constructor -> insert -> find.
        Then, I moved to test if Table worked on edge case testing.
        E.g. I tested if Table can store structs (no), but it can
        store pointers to structs. To be honest, I think i can
        get Table to work with structs, but it was not necessary
        for its brainpower needed to do this.

    main.cpp:
        This my starting point to write the gerp program.
        My writing main to initially only handle
        a simple user argc check, I can then work on my 
        unit testing formula for future gerp tests.

    Gerp.h:
        I tried writing the bare minimum in Gerp so that I
        can start including input files to my gerp unit testing.
        So, the quit function was done first. Then, I moved on 
        to the file change function and the search function.

    After submitting my first draft to gradescope, I got a few errors
    that, as of Aug 6 17:49, I did not figure out. So I needed to do
    more trouble shooting. One thing that bugged me was that I cannot
    test big directories and long tests because they keep timing out.
    For some tests, I verifying by manually running it in my terminal,
    but this is clearly suboptimal.

    I then decided that I needed to make my code faster. After making
    some changes, I ran my old unit test to verify i did not break anything.
    Although my program now takes half the time as my initial version when 
    measured by gerp_perf, it still times out when running certain 
    long tests in unit_tests.h, which is a bummer.

